in our vote for B or change. We need to refer to prior discussion. We have to decide.\n\nWe have to consider all candidates. We have not yet discussed Candidate A and D. But we need to keep coverage. However we are in round 3, we can bring up new candidate or discuss further. The guidelines: still need to cover all candidates before final recommendation. So we could bring up Candidate D or A. But we also need to state our current recommendation. We could shift to Candidate D or A if we think they are better. But we need to base on evidence.\n\nLet's evaluate Candidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, concentrates very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, regarded as arrogant/know-it-all, hot temper, relatively weak leadership skills. Strengths: responsibility, concentration, problem solving, adaptability. Concerns: arrogance, hot temper, weak leadership, possibly crew cooperation issues. No explicit reliability statement. Could be risky.\n\nCandidate A: can anticipate dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities, sometimes not good at taking criticism, can be unorganized, show\u2011off. No reliability, stress, communication info. Leadership is good but show\u2011off may affect crew.\n\nGiven the current leading candidate is C, with strong reliability and decision making, but communication issues. B has reliability (conscientious) but memory and nasty remarks. D has responsibility but temperament issues. A lacks many data.\n\nThus likely the panel will converge on C. As agent_1, we could either maintain B or switch to C. We need to consider the instruction: we should be evidence-based and willing to revise. The discussion so far: Agent 3 argued C is stronger, Agent 2 also argued C is stronger, Agent 1 (us) argued B is stronger. Now we need to respond. We could reconsider and maybe shift to C, acknowledging the arguments. Or we could defend B, but we need to address the concerns about C's communication and egocentric attitude. We could also bring up Candidate D as a new perspective, but we must still give a recommendation.\n\nGiven the strong reliability of C and positive crew atmosphere, and the concerns about B's memory and nasty remarks, I think it's reasonable to shift to C. So we will change our vote to Candidate C, referencing the prior arguments and our own notes.\n\nWe need to include explicit facts: Candidate C 100% reliable, creates positive atmosphere, quick correct decisions, calm in crisis, understands complicated technology. Concerns: difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education. Compare to B's weak memory for numbers and nasty remarks. Argue that C's core safety strengths outweigh B's concerns, and that communication issues can be mitigated.\n\nThus our PUBLIC_MESSAGE: \"I have reconsidered after hearing Agent 3 and Agent 2's points. While I previously favored B, the evidence for C's absolute reliability, positive crew atmosphere, quick correct decisions, calmness, and technical competence outweigh B's memory weakness and interpersonal remarks. Therefore I now vote for Candidate C.\"\n\nMake sure to reference prior discussion. Provide explicit facts. Provide reasoning.\n\nNow produce METADATA_JSON with vote Candidate C.\n\nProceed."}
